 ", which he would go on to win the following day. Despite the time that had elapsed, three participants had won medals at the previous Olympics or World Championships: " [:a {:href "/athletes/11146"} "Ernie Riedel"] " of the United States (bronze in the " [:a {:href "/results/255006"} "1936 K-1 10,000"] "), " [:a {:href "/athletes/9625"} "Henri Eberhardt"] " of France (silver in the " [:a {:href "/results/255021"} "1936 Folding K-1 10,000"] "), and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10523"} "Czesław Sobieraj"] " of Poland (silver in the 1938 K-1 10,000 Worlds). The Swiss entrant, " [:a {:href "/athletes/10681"} "Emil Bottlang"] ", had also competed in the " [:a {:href "/results/255023"} "1936 Folding K-2 10,000"] ", although he had not won a medal."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Due to the fact that the " [:a {:href "/venues/223"} "Henley"] " could only accommodate a limited number of boats, and the " [:a {:href "/organizations/52"} "International Canoe Federation"] " forbade heats in races of this distance, the competitors began at intervals of 30 seconds. According to the " [:strong {} "Official Report"] ", “[t]his decision, although inevitable, was not popular, since it is impossible for a competitor to judge his position relative to the others, and he is forced to race to the clock, an extremely difficult technique”.  Fredriksson was the star of the event and won a well-deserved gold medal, and he was followed across the finish line by Finland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9565"} "Kurt Wires"] " and Norway’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10417"} "Eivind Skabo"] ", who took silver and bronze, respectively. Wires collapsed upon completion of the event and was not made aware of his second-place finish (due to the staggered start) until he was revived by Denmark’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9428"} "Knud Ditlevsen"] ", who came in fourth."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tb